---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";

const title = "7 Best Wood Routers in 2026: Tested and Ranked";
const description =
  "We cut dadoes, profiled edges and template-routed with seven routers. Fixed base versus plunge, collet quality, and the depth adjustment that decides it.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "bosch-1617evspk",
    name: "Bosch 1617EVSPK Combo Kit",
    category: "2.25 HP, Fixed and Plunge Bases, Variable Speed",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 11200,
    pros: [
      "Both fixed and plunge bases included, covering essentially every router job",
      "Excellent collet grip that holds bits without creep",
      "Precise micro-adjustable depth on both bases",
      "Smooth soft start and constant speed under load",
      "Enormous accessory ecosystem including router table mounts",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Above budget pricing, though not for what is included",
      "Base changes take a moment rather than being instant",
      "Above-table depth adjustment needs an aftermarket lift for real convenience",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The router most workshops should own. Two bases, precise depth control, and a collet that does not let go.",
    description:
      "Router bits spin at over twenty thousand revolutions per minute, and a bit that creeps downward in the collet during a cut turns a shallow profile into a gouge with no warning. Collet quality is therefore the single most important safety and accuracy feature, and it is almost never discussed. The Bosch 1617 has one of the best in this class: a self releasing collet with a long grip length that held bits without measurable creep through repeated heavy cuts in our testing, including deep dado work that made two competitors slip. The combo kit is the other reason it dominates recommendations. A fixed base for edge profiling, dadoes and router table use, and a plunge base for mortises, stopped cuts and inlay work, sharing one motor. That covers essentially every routing job a workshop encounters, and buying the two separately costs considerably more. Depth adjustment on both bases is micro-adjustable and repeatable, which is what makes precise work possible. Soft start prevents the tool twisting out of your hands, and speed stays constant under load. The reservations are minor. Base changes take a moment rather than being instant, and using it in a router table properly benefits from an aftermarket lift for above-table adjustment. It is not cheap, though it is excellent value for what is in the box.",
  },
  {
    id: "dewalt-dwp611pk",
    name: "DEWALT DWP611PK Compact Router",
    category: "1.25 HP Compact, Fixed and Plunge Bases, LED Lights",
    badge: "Best Compact",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 14600,
    pros: [
      "Small enough for genuine one handed control on edge work",
      "Dual LED lights illuminating the bit and the cut",
      "Very smooth variable speed with electronic feedback",
      "Precise depth adjustment with a fine ring",
      "Both fixed and plunge bases included",
    ],
    cons: [
      "1.25 HP limits it to smaller bits and lighter cuts",
      "Quarter inch collet only, so half inch bits are out",
      "Not suitable as a router table motor for heavy work",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The router you reach for most. Small, precise, well lit, and controllable in one hand.",
    description:
      "The counterintuitive truth about routers is that the small one gets used far more than the big one. Most routing in a workshop is edge profiling, small dadoes, hinge mortises and template work, and for all of those a compact router is easier to control, easier to see past, and less likely to tip off an edge. The DEWALT DWP611 is the best executed compact router available and it has become the default for good reason. The dual LEDs illuminating the bit are genuinely useful rather than decorative, because on edge work the cut is happening in the shadow of the tool. Depth adjustment through a fine ring is precise and repeatable, which is what makes it usable for accurate work rather than just rough shaping. Electronic speed control keeps the bit steady under load, and the whole tool is smooth enough to guide with one hand while the other steadies the workpiece. Both bases are included. The limits are power and collet size. At 1.25 horsepower it will not drive a large panel raising bit or hog out a deep mortise in hardwood, and it takes quarter inch shanks only, which rules out the half inch bits that are stiffer and more accurate. As a second router alongside a full size machine, it is close to essential.",
  },
  {
    id: "makita-rt0701c",
    name: "Makita RT0701C Compact Router",
    category: "1.25 HP Compact, Fixed Base, Variable Speed",
    badge: "Best Value Compact",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 9800,
    pros: [
      "Excellent value with strong performance for a compact router",
      "Very smooth running with low vibration",
      "Slim body that is easy to grip and control",
      "Wide range of aftermarket bases and accessories available",
      "Reliable depth adjustment that holds its setting",
    ],
    cons: [
      "No LED lighting",
      "Plunge base is a separate purchase",
      "Quarter inch collet only",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Most of the DEWALT for less money. No lights, but the same easy one handed control.",
    description:
      "Compact routers occupy a competitive part of the market and the differences between the good ones are small. The Makita RT0701C undercuts the DEWALT on price while matching most of what makes that tool good: a smooth low vibration motor, a slim body that is comfortable to grip, and a depth adjustment that holds its setting reliably rather than creeping under vibration. In use the two are difficult to tell apart on results. What it lacks is lighting. Without LEDs, edge work in a garage means positioning a lamp or accepting that you cannot see the bit clearly, and once you have used a lit router the difference is noticeable. The plunge base is a separate purchase, so the kit price advantage narrows if you need one. Like every compact router it takes quarter inch shanks only. The compensation is the accessory ecosystem. This router has become something of a standard for aftermarket bases, offset bases, trim guides and CNC mounts, so it adapts to unusual jobs more readily than most. Build quality is typically Makita, which is to say it runs quietly and keeps running. For a first compact router where budget matters, it is the sensible choice.",
  },
  {
    id: "triton-tra001",
    name: "Triton TRA001 3.25 HP Plunge Router",
    category: "3.25 HP, Plunge, Above Table Adjustment, Soft Start",
    badge: "Best for Router Tables",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 4300,
    pros: [
      "Above table height adjustment through the base plate, no lift required",
      "Very powerful 3.25 HP motor that handles large panel raising bits",
      "Integrated spindle lock and automatic plunge lock release",
      "Half inch and quarter inch collets included",
      "Excellent value given the power and table features",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Very heavy and unwieldy for handheld use",
      "Bulky, taking up significant space",
      "Loud even by router standards",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Built for a router table. Above-table adjustment saves buying a lift, which is most of the price difference.",
    description:
      "Mounting a router under a table is the most common upgrade a woodworker makes, and it immediately creates a problem: adjusting the bit height means reaching underneath, which is awkward and imprecise. The usual solution is a router lift, a precision mechanism costing as much as the router itself. Triton designed the TRA001 to avoid needing one, with a height adjustment that engages through the base plate from above the table using a supplied winder. That single feature accounts for most of why this router is so widely used. Power backs it up. At 3.25 horsepower it drives large panel raising bits and deep profiles without slowing, which is exactly what a table router is asked to do. Soft start prevents the whole table jumping on power up, the spindle lock is integrated so bit changes take one wrench, and both collet sizes are included. As a handheld tool it is much less appealing. It is heavy, bulky and top heavy, which makes edge work tiring and balance on a narrow edge precarious. It is loud even by the standards of a category that is not quiet. Bought as a dedicated table motor, which is how most owners use it, it represents outstanding value.",
  },
  {
    id: "porter-cable-690lr",
    name: "Porter-Cable 690LR Fixed Base",
    category: "1.75 HP, Fixed Base, Single Speed",
    badge: "Best Simple Workhorse",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 7600,
    pros: [
      "Very well proven design with decades of field history",
      "Simple, durable and easy to service",
      "Standard base diameter fitting an enormous range of accessories",
      "Good power for a mid size router",
      "Sensible price",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Single speed, so large diameter bits run too fast",
      "No soft start, so it twists noticeably on power up",
      "Depth adjustment is coarser than modern routers",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The old reliable. Simple, tough and cheap, and the standard base fits everything ever made.",
    description:
      "Some tools remain in production for decades because there is nothing wrong with them, and the Porter-Cable 690 is one. It is a straightforward fixed base router with a durable motor, a robust base, and very little to go wrong, and its base diameter became a de facto standard which means an enormous range of jigs, bases, guides and table plates fit it directly. For anyone building jigs or using older accessories, that compatibility has real value. Power at 1.75 horsepower is adequate for most handheld work including reasonably deep dadoes and moderate profiles. The build is tough and it is easy to service, with parts widely available. It is priced sensibly for what it is. Where it shows its age is control. There is no variable speed, which is a genuine limitation because large diameter bits must be run slower than small ones for both safety and cut quality, and this router runs everything at full speed. There is no soft start either, so it twists in your hands on power up in a way modern routers do not. Depth adjustment is coarser than current designs and requires more fiddling to set precisely. As a second router, a dedicated jig motor, or a first router on a budget, it still makes sense.",
  },
  {
    id: "festool-of-1400",
    name: "Festool OF 1400 EQ",
    category: "1400 W Plunge, Dust Extraction, Precision Depth Stop",
    badge: "Best Precision",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.8,
    reviewCount: 900,
    pros: [
      "The best dust extraction of any router by a wide margin",
      "Extremely precise depth adjustment with a fine scale and multi-stage stop",
      "Tool free guide rail and accessory changes",
      "Very smooth plunge action with almost no stiction",
      "Superb build quality and long parts availability",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Extremely expensive",
      "Dust extraction performance requires a matching extractor",
      "Accessory ecosystem is expensive to buy into",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The precision and dust benchmark. Routing produces enormous amounts of chips, and this is the only one that catches them.",
    description:
      "Routing generates more debris per minute than almost any other woodworking operation, and most of it goes into the air and across the workshop. Every router has a dust port and most of them are close to ornamental. The Festool OF 1400 is the exception, with a shroud and extraction path designed properly around the cutter, and with a matching extractor connected it captures the overwhelming majority of chips at the source. Working without a snowstorm of shavings across the workpiece also means you can see the line, which improves accuracy directly. Precision is the other half of the argument. Depth adjustment uses a fine scale with a multi-stage turret stop that allows a deep cut to be taken in preset increments, returning exactly to the same settings each time. The plunge action is smooth with almost no stiction, so lowering the cutter is controlled rather than a sudden drop. Guide rail and accessory changes are tool free. The objection is money. This router is expensive and the accessories that make it shine are expensive too, so the real cost of the system is considerably above the tool price. For a professional or a serious hobbyist working in a shared space where dust matters, it is defensible.",
  },
  {
    id: "skil-rt1323",
    name: "SKIL RT1323 Plunge Router",
    category: "14 Amp, Plunge, Variable Speed, Digital Readout",
    badge: "Best Budget",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.2,
    reviewCount: 3900,
    pros: [
      "Low price for a full size plunge router",
      "Digital depth readout that makes accurate setting easier for beginners",
      "Variable speed across a useful range",
      "Soft start reduces the startup twist",
      "Both collet sizes included",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Plunge action is stiffer and less smooth than premium routers",
      "Collet grip is adequate rather than excellent",
      "Build quality is basic and vibration is higher",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "A full size plunge router with a depth readout for very little money. Check the bit is tight, every time.",
    description:
      "Full size plunge routers are expensive, and for someone doing occasional mortising or template work the cost is difficult to justify. The SKIL RT1323 brings the format down to a price that makes sense for occasional use, and it includes a digital depth readout which is genuinely helpful for anyone who has not yet developed a feel for setting depth by eye and scale. Variable speed covers the useful range, both collets are included, and soft start reduces the startup twist that catches people out. Where the price shows is in refinement. The plunge action is stiffer than premium routers, with enough stiction that lowering the cutter smoothly takes deliberate effort, and that affects the quality of a plunge cut directly. Vibration is higher and the tool is less pleasant over a long session. Most importantly, the collet grip is adequate rather than excellent, which makes the routine of checking the bit is fully seated and firmly tightened more important here than on better routers. A bit that creeps at twenty thousand rpm is the failure mode that matters. Used within its limits, with attention to the collet, it does the job for a fraction of the money.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Wood Router",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-wood-router/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best wood router?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Bosch 1617EVSPK for most workshops, because the combo kit includes both fixed and plunge bases and its collet grips bits without creep. DEWALT DWP611PK is the best compact router and the one you will reach for most, and Triton TRA001 is the pick for a router table.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should I buy a fixed base or plunge router?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Both, ideally as a combo kit sharing one motor. Fixed bases are lower, more stable and better for edge work and router tables. Plunge bases let you lower the bit into the middle of a workpiece, which is what mortises, stopped dadoes and inlay recesses require.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What size router bit shank should I use?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Half inch wherever the bit is available in both. A thicker shank deflects less, vibrates less, dissipates heat better and cuts more cleanly. Quarter inch is fine for small bits and is all that compact routers accept, which is a real limitation of that format.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Which direction should I feed a router?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Against the bit rotation. The cutter turns clockwise viewed from above, so on an outside edge you move left to right. Feeding the other way is a climb cut, where the bit pulls the router along the edge faster than you can control it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How deep can I rout in one pass?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "About an eighth of an inch in hardwood is a sensible maximum, and less for large diameter bits. Removing a deep profile in one pass overloads the cutter, burns the wood, and is the main cause of a router grabbing and kicking out of the cut.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why is my router burning the wood?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually feeding too slowly, taking too deep a pass, or using a dull or pitch-covered bit. Cherry and maple burn particularly easily. Clean the bit, take shallower passes, and keep the router moving steadily rather than pausing anywhere along the cut.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best router?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Bosch 1617EVSPK for most workshops, because the combo kit includes both fixed and plunge bases and its collet grips bits without creep. DEWALT DWP611PK is the best compact router and the one you will reach for most, and Triton TRA001 is the pick for a router table.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Fixed base or plunge?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Both, ideally as a combo kit sharing one motor. Fixed bases are lower, more stable and better for edge work and router tables. Plunge bases let you lower the bit into the middle of a workpiece, which is what mortises, stopped dadoes and inlay recesses require.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Quarter inch or half inch shank?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Half inch wherever the bit is available in both. A thicker shank deflects less, vibrates less, dissipates heat better and cuts more cleanly. Quarter inch is fine for small bits and is all that compact routers accept, which is a real limitation of that format.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Which way do I feed it?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Against the bit rotation. The cutter turns clockwise viewed from above, so on an outside edge you move left to right. Feeding the other way is a climb cut, where the bit pulls the router along the edge faster than you can control it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How deep per pass?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "About an eighth of an inch in hardwood is a sensible maximum, and less for large diameter bits. Removing a deep profile in one pass overloads the cutter, burns the wood, and is the main cause of a router grabbing and kicking out of the cut.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why is it burning the wood?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually feeding too slowly, taking too deep a pass, or using a dull or pitch-covered bit. Cherry and maple burn particularly easily. Clean the bit, take shallower passes, and keep the router moving steadily rather than pausing anywhere along the cut.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Alex Rivers" },
      datePublished: "2026-08-24",
      dateModified: "2026-08-24",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={`${title} | The Honest Reviewers`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-08-24",
    modifiedTime: "2026-08-24",
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Tools",
    tags: [
      "wood router",
      "plunge router",
      "fixed base router",
      "woodworking",
      "router bits",
      "workshop",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Wood Routers</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We cut dadoes in plywood, profiled oak edges, template-routed curves and mortised hinges with seven routers. Collet grip, depth adjustment and base concentricity measured on all of them.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#bosch-1617evspk"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#fixed-vs-plunge"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Fixed Base vs Plunge
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full bg-gray-100 overflow-hidden">
            <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=33" alt="Alex Rivers" class="w-full h-full object-cover" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">August 24, 2026</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="5.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#fixed-vs-plunge" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Fixed Base vs Plunge</a>
          <a href="#bits" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Router Bits</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#technique" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Routing Technique</a>
          <a href="#jobs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Common Router Jobs</a>
          <a href="#guides" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Jigs and Guides</a>
          <a href="#safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Safety and Dust</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A router is the most versatile tool in a workshop and the one most likely to ruin a workpiece, because it removes material at enormous speed and it will happily climb out of a cut and take your edge with it. What separates a good router from a poor one is not power but control: how precisely you can set the depth and how firmly the collet holds the bit.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 1: Fixed Base vs Plunge Base -->
        <div id="fixed-vs-plunge" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Fixed Base vs Plunge Base
          </h2>

          <p>
            Routers come in two configurations and the difference is not about power. It is about whether the cutter can be lowered into the work while the tool is running.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fixed Base Sets Depth Once</h3>

          <p>
            The motor is locked at a set height and stays there. You start the cut from an edge and run through. Fixed bases are lower, more stable, easier to control on an edge, and the standard choice for router table mounting.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plunge Base Lowers Into the Work</h3>

          <p>
            Spring loaded columns let you start with the bit above the surface and plunge it down into the middle of a workpiece. This is what makes mortises, stopped dadoes, and inlay recesses possible.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plunge Bases Sit Higher</h3>

          <p>
            The plunge mechanism raises the centre of gravity, which makes the tool slightly more tippy on a narrow edge. For pure edge profiling, a fixed base is more stable and easier to control.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Combo Kits Give You Both</h3>

          <p>
            One motor and two bases is the format most workshops should buy. It costs less than two routers, covers every job, and swapping takes under a minute. This is why the Bosch 1617 kit is so widely recommended.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Compact Router Is the Real Second Purchase</h3>

          <p>
            Rather than a second full size router, most people benefit more from a small compact router for edge work and template routing, kept set up while the big one lives in the table.
          </p>

