        <!-- SECTION 2: Router Bits and Shank Size -->
        <div id="bits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Router Bits and Shank Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bits are where routing accuracy and safety are decided, and the choices matter more than the router does.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Half inch shanks are stiffer than quarter inch.</strong> A thicker shank deflects less, vibrates less and cuts more cleanly, and it dissipates heat better. Where a bit is available in both, buy the half inch version.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Carbide tipped is the minimum.</strong> High speed steel bits dull very quickly in anything harder than pine, and a dull bit burns rather than cuts. Solid carbide is better again for small diameter bits where a brazed tip would be fragile.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Large diameter bits must run slower.</strong> The rim speed of a big panel raising bit at full router speed is dangerous and produces a poor cut. Variable speed exists for this, and a router without it should not be used with large bits.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Bearing guided bits follow an edge or template.</strong> A bearing riding on the workpiece or a template controls the cut, which is what makes template routing and edge profiling straightforward. Keep bearings clean and check they spin freely.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Seat the bit properly, then back it out slightly.</strong> Pushing the shank fully to the bottom of the collet and tightening there can distort the grip. Insert fully, withdraw about a sixteenth of an inch, then tighten firmly.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Clean pitch off bits regularly.</strong> Resin build-up increases friction, causes burning, and makes a sharp bit behave like a dull one. Pitch remover and a brass brush restore most bits to nearly new performance.
          </p>
        </div>

