        <!-- SECTION 4: Routing Without Ruining the Workpiece -->
        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Routing Without Ruining the Workpiece
          </h2>

          <p>
            A router removes material fast enough that a mistake is instantaneous, and the technique that prevents it is a short list.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Feed against the rotation.</strong> The bit spins clockwise viewed from above, so on an outside edge you move the router left to right. Going the other way is a climb cut, where the bit pulls the router along and control is lost.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Take shallow passes.</strong> An eighth of an inch per pass is a sensible maximum in hardwood. Trying to remove a deep profile in one pass overloads the bit, burns the wood, and is how routers grab and kick.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Keep both hands on the router and the work clamped.</strong> Holding a workpiece with one hand while routing with the other is how people get hurt. Clamps, a bench stop, or a non-slip mat free both hands for the tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Let the bit reach full speed before contact.</strong> Starting a router with the bit touching the wood makes it grab and jump. Start clear, let it spin up, then move into the cut.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Rout end grain first on a four sided profile.</strong> End grain blows out at the exit corner. Doing the ends first means the subsequent long grain passes remove that tear-out, leaving clean corners.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check the bit is tight before every session.</strong> A bit creeping down in the collet during a cut is the failure that ruins workpieces and injures people. Two seconds with the wrench each time is the whole precaution.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Left to Right on an Outside Edge</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              The bit turns clockwise seen from above, so feeding the router left to right along an outside edge makes the cutter push back against your feed direction, which is controllable. Reverse that and the bit grabs and runs, dragging the router along the edge faster than you can stop it. Inside a frame or an opening, the direction reverses.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: What a Router Actually Does -->
        <div id="jobs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What a Router Actually Does
          </h2>

          <p>
            The router is the most versatile tool in a workshop, and the breadth is worth spelling out because most owners use a fraction of it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Edge profiling.</strong> Roundovers, chamfers, coves and ogees on the edge of a board, using a bearing guided bit that follows the edge. This is what most people buy a router for and it is the easiest job it does.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Dadoes, grooves and rabbets.</strong> Slots for shelves, grooves for panels and rabbets for backs, cut with a straight bit against a guide. This is the joinery half of the tool and it is where a router replaces a table saw for many people.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Template routing.</strong> A bearing bit following a template reproduces any shape exactly, as many times as needed. This is how identical curved parts get made without a bandsaw and hours of sanding.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Mortises and hinge recesses.</strong> A plunge router with a guide bush cuts flat bottomed recesses precisely, which is far faster and more accurate than chiselling hinge mortises by hand.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Flattening slabs and levelling.</strong> With a sled and a wide bit, a router flattens a slab too wide for any planer. It is slow and it works, and it is the standard method for live edge work.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Inlay, lettering and decorative work.</strong> With the right bits and jigs a router cuts inlay recesses, engraves lettering and produces decorative details that would otherwise require hand tools and considerably more skill.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Jigs, Guides and Router Tables -->
        <div id="guides" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Jigs, Guides and Router Tables
          </h2>

          <p>
            A router freehand is a shaping tool. A router referenced against something is a precision machine, and the difference is entirely in the guide you give it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Bearing guided bits are the simplest reference.</strong> A bearing above or below the cutter rides on the workpiece edge or a template, so the cut follows that surface exactly. Most edge profiling and all template routing works this way and needs no setup at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>An edge guide handles cuts parallel to an edge.</strong> A fence attached to the base rides along the workpiece edge, keeping a groove or rabbet a set distance in. It is the accessory most routers include and the one most owners never fit.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A clamped straightedge is often better than any accessory.</strong> Running the router base against a batten clamped to the work gives a dead straight cut anywhere on a panel, and it costs nothing. Allow for the offset between the bit and the edge of the base.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Guide bushes let a plunge router follow a template.</strong> A collar around the bit rides inside a template opening, which is how hinge jigs, letter templates and dovetail jigs work. Remember the bush is larger than the bit, so the template must be undersized accordingly.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A router table inverts everything.</strong> With the router under a table and the bit projecting up through it, you move the work instead of the tool. Small parts, repeated profiles and anything needing a fence become far easier and considerably safer.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Fence quality decides a router table.</strong> A flat fence that adjusts squarely and locks without shifting matters more than the table surface or the router underneath it. A poor fence makes an expensive table useless for accurate work.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Router Safety and Dust -->
        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Router Safety and Dust
          </h2>

          <p>
            Routers are among the more dangerous workshop tools because the cutter is exposed, fast, and capable of grabbing.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Eye protection is not optional.</strong> Routers throw chips at high speed directly toward the operator, and a carbide fragment from a chipped bit travels further and harder. Safety glasses every time, without exception.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hearing protection matters more than people think.</strong> Routers run at very high speed and are among the loudest tools in a workshop. Cumulative exposure over years is what causes damage, not any single session.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Router dust is fine and abundant.</strong> The cutting action produces a large volume of fine particulate, and hardwood dust is classified as a carcinogen in several jurisdictions. Dust extraction plus a rated respirator is the correct approach.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Never adjust anything with the router plugged in.</strong> Changing a bit, adjusting depth, or clearing a jam with power connected is how serious injuries happen. Unplug, then work on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Watch for the router tipping on a narrow edge.</strong> Profiling a thin edge leaves most of the base unsupported, and a tip mid-cut gouges the workpiece and can lose control. An offset base or a wider support surface prevents it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Support the router fully before switching it off.</strong> A bit continues spinning for several seconds after the trigger is released, and setting a router down on a bench with the cutter still turning marks the bench, damages the bit, and can walk the tool off the edge. Hold it clear until it stops completely.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Keep loose clothing and sleeves clear.</strong> A spinning bit catches fabric instantly. Long sleeves, cuffs and anything hanging should be secured before the tool is switched on.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8: Frequently Asked Questions -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best router?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Bosch 1617EVSPK for most workshops, because the combo kit includes both fixed and plunge bases and its collet grips bits without creep. DEWALT DWP611PK is the best compact router and the one you will reach for most, and Triton TRA001 is the pick for a router table.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Fixed base or plunge?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both, ideally as a combo kit sharing one motor. Fixed bases are lower, more stable and better for edge work and router tables. Plunge bases let you lower the bit into the middle of a workpiece, which is what mortises, stopped dadoes and inlay recesses require.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Quarter inch or half inch shank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Half inch wherever the bit is available in both. A thicker shank deflects less, vibrates less, dissipates heat better and cuts more cleanly. Quarter inch is fine for small bits and is all that compact routers accept, which is a real limitation of that format.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which way do I feed it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Against the bit rotation. The cutter turns clockwise viewed from above, so on an outside edge you move left to right. Feeding the other way is a climb cut, where the bit pulls the router along the edge faster than you can control it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How deep per pass?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                About an eighth of an inch in hardwood is a sensible maximum, and less for large diameter bits. Removing a deep profile in one pass overloads the cutter, burns the wood, and is the main cause of a router grabbing and kicking out of the cut.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is it burning the wood?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually feeding too slowly, taking too deep a pass, or using a dull or pitch-covered bit. Cherry and maple burn particularly easily. Clean the bit, take shallower passes, and keep the router moving steadily rather than pausing anywhere along the cut.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Router Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Router mistakes are fast and expensive, and nearly all of them come from the same handful of habits.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Routing in the Wrong Direction</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Feeding with the bit rotation instead of against it produces a climb cut, where the cutter grabs the wood and pulls the router along the edge faster than you can stop it. On an outside edge, move left to right; inside an opening, the direction reverses. Getting this wrong is the classic router accident.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taking Too Much in One Pass</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Trying to cut a deep profile in a single pass overloads the bit, burns the wood, strains the motor and is the main cause of a router kicking out of the cut. An eighth of an inch per pass in hardwood is sensible, and less for large diameter bits.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Checking the Collet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bit creeping downward in the collet during a cut turns a shallow profile into a gouge instantly, and at twenty thousand rpm there is no warning. Seat the shank fully, withdraw it a fraction, tighten firmly, and check it before every session.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Holding the Workpiece by Hand</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Routing with one hand while holding the work with the other means neither the tool nor the workpiece is properly controlled, and a router that grabs will move both. Clamps, bench dogs or a non-slip mat cost almost nothing and free both hands for the tool.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

